The target uses r12 (ip) to hold arg0 across the whole body and keeps the 0x03000830 base in r5, reusing r5 for both stores. Mine recomputes offsets. Tried: struct S_03000830 with field_0x04/field_0x06 (2335, worse — two literal loads), vu16* r5 local (2090), extern u16 D_03000830[4] (2090), 0x3F40 literal (2090), declaration order r2/r4 vs r4/r2 (2090). The struct/offset form regresses because agbcc emits base+offset with a fresh literal; the array form is best. Next agent: try `extern vu16 D_03000830[];` and access D_03000830[2]/[3], or force arg0 into a local pointer variable declared first.

Best 305/78 insns (96.0%). This is the same family as sub_0807B12C/sub_08082FA0 but with an extra field-0x28 indirection. Working C:
  struct S_02025FF0 { u8 _[0x18C2]; u16 w; }; extern struct S_02025FF0 D_02025FF0;
  struct S_080766E4 { u8 _[0x14]; u32 field_0x14; u8 _[0x18]; s16 field_0x22; u32 field_0x24; u32 field_0x28; u8 _[0xC]; u32 field_0x38; };
  void *temp; u8 *p;
  if (sub_0800168C(arg0,0,0x68,3)==0) { sub_080C31E0(arg0,(void*)arg0->field_0x24); return; }
  if ((temp = sub_0800355C())==NULL) { sub_0806D2D0(arg0); return; }
  arg0->field_0x38 = (u32)temp;
  *(u32*)((u8*)temp+0x1C) = 0x80000004;
  *(u16*)((u8*)temp+0x02) = (u16)((0x10 & *(u16*)(*(u32*)((u8*)arg0->field_0x28+0x38)+0x02)) | *(u16*)((u8*)temp+0x02));
  *(u16*)temp = D_02025FF0.w;
  *(u8*)((u8*)arg0 + arg0->field_0x22*2 + 0x19) = 0;
  p = (u8*)arg0 + 0x18;
  sub_080C31E4(p, (u8*)arg0+0x22, *(u32*)0x08671BB0);
  *(u8*)((u8*)arg0 + arg0->field_0x22*2 + 0x19) += 1;
  sub_080C31E0(arg0, *(void**)((u32)p[arg0->field_0x22*2]*4 + arg0->field_0x14));
Remaining: 7 regalloc + literal-load scheduling (target loads 0x08671BB0 into r2 then ldr r2,[r2]; mine uses r0). Tried: u16 f2 local (550, worse), u32 t28 local for field_0x28+0x38 (970, worse), extern u32 D_08671BB0 (no change), declaration order p,temp vs temp,p (no change). The field_0x28+0x38 chain must be written as arg0->field_0x28 then +0x38 as a separate deref to match the target's two ldr's.

Best attempt (score 215, 18-row body byte-identical): 
  void sub_080186DC(u16 *a, s16 b) { s32 v = *a - b; *a = (u16)v; if ((s32)((u16)v << 16) <= 0x03E70000) *a = 1000; }
The ENTIRE body matches the target instruction-for-instruction (adds r2,r0; ldrh; lsls/asrs b; subs; strh; lsls r0,#16; ldr r1,=0x03E70000; cmp; bgt; movs #0xFA/lsls #2; adds; strh). The ONLY remaining diff is the compiler emitting a non-leaf prologue `push {lr}` ... `pop {r0}; bx r0` where the target is a leaf ending `bx lr` (bgt displacement 0x1e vs 0x1c as a consequence). Tried: early return, `(v<<16) < 0x03E80000` (worse, 415 — added 0xFFFFFFFF literal), `s16 v` local with `v <= 999` (680). search_flags: all 5 agbcc flag variants give 215. try_compiler with agbcc-own-b3673d20 errors out. The sibling sub_080186C0 has the identical prologue problem (best 215 there too, 12 attempts by another agent), so this looks like an agbcc 2.95 limitation for this clamp-with-branch pattern in this build; the original may have used a different toolchain revision. Needs a C formulation that makes agbcc emit leaf `bx lr` with a conditional store, or the alternate compiler working.

Best score 170/22 insns. Best source:
  void sub_08018410(void *arg0) { u8 *base; void **p; void *q; s32 i; base = *(u8 **)((u8 *)arg0+0x3C); p = (void **)(base+0x80); i = 9; do { q=*p; if(q!=0){ *(u16*)((u8*)q+0x64) |= 1; *p = 0; } p++; i--; } while (i>=0); }
This version already has ALL registers right (r5=1, r4=0, r2=p, r3=i) and 22 instructions. Remaining diff: (1) target pushes/pops an extra r6 that is never used (`push {r4,r5,r6,lr}` vs mine `push {r4,r5,lr}`) — likely a compiler artifact of a source form I haven't found; (2) instruction order: target emits `ldr r0,[r0,#0x3c]; movs r5,#1; movs r4,#0; adds r2,r0,#0; adds r2,#0x80; movs r3,#9`, mine emits the base/p init first then the constants. Introducing s32 locals `one=1, zero=0` fixes the order but corrupts regalloc (one→r6, p→r3, i→r4, plus an extra `adds r0,r6,#0`, score 185). Tried declaring them in a nested block (same 185), zero-only local (440). Needs a C form that materializes the constants right after the base load while keeping p in r2 / i in r3, and that makes agbcc save r6.

Best 160/25 insns (93.6%). Source:
  s32 sub_08014A6C(void *arg0, s32 arg1, s32 arg2, s32 arg3) { s32 result; result = sub_080059DC(*(s32*)((u8*)arg0+0x10), *(s32*)((u8*)arg0+0x14)); if (result - arg2 <= arg3) { sub_080146D0(arg0,arg1,arg3); return 1; } sub_080146D0(arg0,arg1,result - arg2); return 0; }
All logic and 8 leading instructions match. Remaining: target emits an extra `adds r2,r0,#0` copy of the sub_080059DC return value into r2 before `subs r2,r2,r4` (mine fuses to `subs r2,r0,r4` — one instruction shorter). Then argument setup order differs: target sets r2 (third arg) BEFORE r0/r1 for both calls; mine sets r0/r1 then r2. Tried separate result/diff locals, a `lim` local, and computing `result - arg2` twice inline — all score 160 unchanged. Needs an agbcc scheduling trick to force the redundant copy and the right-to-left argument evaluation. The 'call sub_080146D0 where original calls something else' message is the usual veneer artifact from the differing code layout.

Best 240/40 insns (94%). Source (m2c-style, no pointer locals):
  void sub_080722E0(void *arg0, u8 arg1) { u8 idx = arg1 & 0x7F; s32 r3 = *(s32 *)(D_0866EF18 + *(u8 *)(D_0866F01C + idx)*4); s32 r2; if (arg1 & 0x80) r3 = -r3; r2 = *(s32 *)(D_0866EF18 + *(u8 *)(D_0866F01C + idx + 0x40)*4); if ((u8)(arg1-0x41) <= 0x7E) r2 = -r2; *(u32*)((u8*)arg0+8)=r2; *(u32*)((u8*)arg0+0xC)=r3; }
All 40 instructions present; only regalloc differs: target r5=arg0, r6=D_0866EF18 base, r4=D_0866F01C table, and loads `ldr r6,=base` BEFORE `ldr r4,=table`; mine r6=arg0, r4=base, r5=table with base literal loaded second (late, after the `ands`). Also target has `adds r0,r2,#0` before the `+0x40` table index where mine reuses r4. Tried every pointer-local declaration order (base/tbl/self, tbl/self/base, etc.) — either 240 (no locals) or 600 (only two callee-saved regs). Needs an agbcc regalloc trick to get r5=self, r6=base, r4=tbl and the early base literal load.

Best 880/32 insns. Source:
  struct S_02021E90_H { u8 _0x000[0x1360]; u8 f1360; u8 _0x1361[0x4D]; u8 f13AE; }; extern struct S_02021E90_H D_02021E90;
  void sub_08062788(void *arg0) { struct S_02021E90_H *base = &D_02021E90; u16 t = *(u16*)((u8*)arg0+0x22); base->f13AE = 0x10 - t; base->f1360 = 6; if ((s16)t == 0x10) { base->f1360 = 0; *(u16*)((u8*)arg0+0x22)=0; *(u32*)((u8*)arg0+0x14)=(u32)sub_080627C8; } }
Offsets and strength reduction are right (0x13AE literal load, 0x9B<<5 for 0x1360, const 0x080627C9). Remaining: (1) target loads base into r3 BEFORE `ldrh r1,[r4,#0x22]`, ours loads base into r2 after the ldrh into r3 (register rotation: target base=r3, t=r1; ours base=r2, t=r3); (2) target keeps `lsls r1,#0x10; asrs r1,#0x10` before `cmp r1,#0x10` (sign-extends t for the comparison even though 0x10 is a small positive constant) — agbcc optimizes my `(s16)t == 0x10` into a plain u16 cmp, removing those two instructions. Tried s16 local, s32 lim local; no change. (3) trailing garbage rows are the ROM literal-pool bytes at 0x080627C4/0x080627C8 being disassembled as instructions — likely a boundary artifact. Needs a C form whose signed comparison agbcc won't fold, and the right register order.

Best 2020/46 insns (56%), obtained with early-return form:
  extern u8 D_02025FF0[];
  s32 sub_080A89A4(void *arg0) { u8 *base = D_02025FF0; s32 v; if (*(u8*)((u8*)arg0+0x4C)==0) { v=*(s32*)(base+0x174C); if(v>0x0087FFFF){ if(v>0x01780000) return 0; return 1;} return 0;} v=*(s32*)(base+0x174C); if(v>0x0087FFFF){ if(v>0x01780000) return 0; return 1;} return 2; }
Core comparisons/constants are right: 0x0087FFFF and 0xBC<<17 (=0x01780000), flag at +0x4C, v at D_02025FF0+0x174C. Cannot reach the target's leaf form: agbcc always emits `push {lr}` ... `pop {r1}; bx r1` for this multi-return shape, whereas the target is a leaf ending `bx lr`. It also hoists the D_02025FF0 literal load to function entry (into r1) instead of re-loading it inside each of the two branches as `ldr r0,=0x02025FF0; ldr r1,=0x174C; adds r0,r0,r1; ldr r1,[r0]`. Tried single-result-variable, nested if/else, ternary, goto labels, and struct-based field access (which fails linking: D_02025FF0_174C is not a known symbol). The `ldrsh r0,[r6,r7]; lsls r2,r0,#8` rows in the diff are the ROM's literal-pool bytes being misdisassembled and reflect our different pool layout. Needs an agbcc trick for a leaf with two duplicated literal-load blocks.

Best 425/28 insns is held by autopilot's earlier m2c attempt (with M2C_DEFINES boilerplate); my closest reproduction scored 825:
  extern u8 D_020279E8[]; s32 sub_0808F040(void) { s16 v = *(s16 *)D_020279E8; switch (v) { case 0: return 0; case 2: return 1; case 6: return 2; case 12: return 3; default: return -1; } }
All comparisons/constants and the binary-search-shaped beq/bgt chain are correct. Remaining: agbcc emits `push {lr}` ... `pop {r1}; bx r1` where target is leaf `bx lr`, so every branch displacement is off by 2-4; also literal offset #0x14 vs target #0x10. Tried switch with per-case returns, switch with single result variable, and if/else chain; none beat 825. Needs an agbcc trick for a leaf switch with 5 return blocks (same prologue problem seen on sub_080186DC/sub_080A89A4).

Best 825/30 insns (72.5%). Source:
  extern void sub_0808EA70(void); void _call_via_r1(void*,void*);
  void sub_0808EA30(void *arg0) { u32 zero = 0; void *p; void *self = arg0; p = *(void**)(*(void**)((u8*)self+0x28)+0x38); if (p == 0) { _call_via_r1(self, *(void**)((u8*)self+0x24)); return; } *(u16*)((u8*)p+4) = 0x100; *(u16*)((u8*)(*(void**)(*(void**)((u8*)self+0x28)+0x38))+6) = 0x100; *(u16*)((u8*)self+2) |= 0x800; *(u16*)((u8*)self+0x22) = zero; *(u32*)((u8*)self+0x14) = (u32)sub_0808EA70; }
All fields/constants are right (0x100, 0x800, +0x38/+0x28/+0x24/+0x14/+0x22/+2). Remaining is regalloc/scheduling: target self in r3 with `push {r4,lr}`, zero in r2 materialized at the top of the else branch (`movs r2,#0`), 0x100 in r1, 0x800 in r4 with `adds r0,r4,#0; orrs r0,r1`; mine self in r2 with `push {lr}`, zero materialized late as `movs r0,#0`, 0x100 in r3 with an extra `adds r0,r3,#0`. Tried a `u32 val800` local (worse, 1010), m2c draft shape (825). Needs a C form that forces r4 to hold 0x800 and the zero constant early.

Best score 10/21 insns. Working C (modeled on m2c best attempt):
